WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.333
Scenario: A workstation displays a ransom note, and several

2
00:00:03.333 --> 00:00:06.667
shared files are becoming inaccessible. The user asks whether

3
00:00:06.667 --> 00:00:10.000
to reboot the machine and delete the note. Safest

4
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:13.333
response: Option A: Isolate affected systems in a coordinated

5
00:00:13.333 --> 00:00:16.667
manner, activate the response plan and preserve evidence; follow

6
00:00:16.667 --> 00:00:20.000
recovery guidance from authorized responders. Containment is urgent, but

7
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:23.333
should be coordinated; evidence and recovery decisions must be

8
00:00:23.333 --> 00:00:26.667
handled through incident response. Option B is unsafe because

9
00:00:26.667 --> 00:00:30.000
Connecting removable media to a potentially infected machine may

10
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.333
expose backups to compromise. Option C is unsafe because

11
00:00:33.333 --> 00:00:36.667
Uncoordinated rebooting may destroy volatile evidence and does not

12
00:00:36.667 --> 00:00:40.000
reliably stop propagation. Option D is unsafe because Deleting

13
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.333
evidence is harmful, and payment is not a reliable

14
00:00:43.333 --> 00:00:46.667
recovery strategy or unilateral user decision. In practice: Activate

15
00:00:46.667 --> 00:00:50.000
the incident-response process, isolate affected systems as directed, protect

16
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:53.333
clean backups, preserve evidence and involve system owners and

17
00:00:53.333 --> 00:00:56.667
authorities as appropriate. Key takeaway: Contain ransomware safely and

18
00:00:56.667 --> 00:01:00.000
coordinate response; do not improvise recovery or destroy evidence.
